  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) The paper aims to develop and evaluate MFM-300(In) as a crystalline MOF nanoreactor for confining white phosphorus, promoting its polymerization into a stable and crystallographically characterizable P8-based double-helical phosphorus chain, relating that motif to amorphous red phosphorus building units, and motivating phosphorus-doped MOF semiconductor routes.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) The retained and repaired conclusions collectively connect a materials need, host-confinement strategy, diffraction-based structural characterization, P8-chain synthesis targets, and relevance to amorphous red phosphorus and phosphorus–MOF semiconductor design."];
  "R11" [label="(0,0,0) Examples from CuI adducts, activated carbon, and CNT hosts support the general pattern that host confinement can stabilize, adsorb, or promote polymerization of phosphorus species."];
  "R12" [label="(0,0,0) Given that host-stabilized P8-based helical chains are a reasonable synthesis target and that MOFs are suitable porous hosts for including P4, potential polymerization, and diffraction analysis, targeting P8-based helical phosphorus chains in a MOF is justified."];
  "R13" [label="(0,0,0) Because red phosphorus is stable but has a wide bandgap and decreasing that bandgap is challenging, investigating new stable polymeric phosphorus forms is a plausible route toward improved properties."];
  "R2" [label="(0,0,0) Because phosphorene has attractive tunable semiconducting behavior while black-phosphorus-based materials face instability or costly synthesis concerns, it is motivated to investigate more stable and accessible phosphorus-based semiconducting materials."];
  "R3" [label="(0,0,0) Porous crystalline hosts can absorb guest species and support single-crystal diffraction analysis of host–guest structures, making them useful platforms for structural determination without separately crystallizing the guest molecule."];
  "R4" [label="(0,0,0) MOFs are promising crystalline porous hosts for including P4, potentially promoting light-driven polymerization, and analyzing host–guest products by diffraction."];
  "R6" [label="(0,0,0) Because amorphous host-derived phosphorus materials have elusive structures, using diffraction-capable porous crystalline hosts is a plausible strategy for obtaining structural information about confined phosphorus species."];
  "n_i1" [label="(0,0,0) If an allotrope/material is unstable in ambient conditions or costly to synthesize, then it is less practical for scalable applications, motivating alternative stable/accessible phosphorus-based semiconducting materials."];
  "n_i3" [label="(0,0,0) If predicted P8-based helical/double-helical chains are more stable than P4, and porous hosts can stabilize polymeric phosphorus chains, then attempting host-stabilized P8-based helical chains is a reasonable synthesis target."];
  "n_i6" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Given that unstable low-nuclearity molecular phosphorus is impractical and that host-stabilized/chain forms can change properties (including band structure), therefore pursuing host-stabilized phosphorus chains/composites is motivated for improved semiconducting behavior."];
  "n_i7" [label="(0,0,0) abduction-reasoning: Given that Raman of the adduct resembles red P and that if amorphous red P contains similar helical P8-chain units then similar Raman features would appear, therefore amorphous red P likely contains such helical chain building units."];
